index 00000000..b0f71554
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Servex/doc/readme-chain.md
@@ -0,0 +1,95 @@
+# The readme chain
+
+**A fresh agent Servex spawns FOR a directory or a page opens already having
+read the readme.md chain from the repo root down to that directory, in order**
+— the owner's own ask, 2026-09-29 — so it knows "where it is" before it does
+anything. `Servex/agents/readme-chain.js` builds that chain; `Servex/agents/Agents.js`
+and `Servex/agents/Layers.js` prepend it to a fresh agent's first message.
+
+## What it does
+
+`readme_chain(dir)` walks from the repo root down to `dir`, one path segment at
+a time (root, then `public/`, then `public/framework/`, …), and collects the
+`readme.md` (or `README.md`) at every level that has one. A level with no
+readme is skipped, not left as a gap. Each readme is cut to its **first
+screen**: up to its own `## More` heading, or its first 40 lines, whichever
+comes first — the rest is left for the agent to read on demand, at the path
+the entry names. It never reads the root `CLAUDE.md`: that file is already in
+every agent's context a different way (the SDK's own project-instructions
+load), so this would just say it twice.
+
+`readme_chain(dir)` returns the raw data — `[{path, text, truncated}]`, root
+to leaf — for a caller who wants the list itself, not formatted words.
+
+`first_prompt(dir, extras = [])` is the seam: it formats the chain as one
+block starting `Where you are: readmes from the root down to <dir>`, applies a
+rough **3,000-token cap** (chars/4; cutting whole readmes from the **top** —
+the most general one, root first — so what is closest to `dir` survives
+longest, and always keeping at least `dir`'s own readme if it has one), then
+appends each of `extras` (`{label, text}`) as its own `## <label>` section
+after the chain. `task-placement`'s "Recent sessions" block, or anything else
+that wants to add its own section to a fresh agent's opening context, calls
+`first_prompt(dir, [{label: "Recent sessions", text: "…"}])` and never has to
+touch this file's formatting or the token cap.
+
+Both are plain functions, no class, and never throw — a readme this can't
+read (permissions, a race, whatever) is just skipped, same as a level with no
+readme at all.
+
+## Where it's wired in
+
+- **`Agents.spawn(spec)`** (`Servex/agents/Agents.js`) — a FRESH spawn (never a
+  `resume`, never one bringing its own `spec.system`) whose directory it can
+  name gets `first_prompt(dir)` prepended, ahead of the skill-load preamble
+  and the caller's own prompt. The directory comes from `spec.task.dir` (a
+  task mastermind, or any agent opened with `task: {dir, card, brief}` —
+  `spawn_agent`'s `task` option and `Dispatcher.js`'s carded tasks both work
+  this way) or `spec.page` (a page-bound agent; a site URL path like
+  `/framework/ux/Dictate/`, mapped onto the matching repo dir under `public/`).
+  Neither present — a plain minion, most forks, the fast assistant, a master/
+  sub-mastermind revival — and `directory_of()` returns `null`: nothing is
+  added, on purpose (see "Left out" below).
+- **`Layers.js`'s card assistant and manager** — `assistant(card)` and
+  `ask_manager(...)` build their FRESH-spawn prompt as a closure
+  (`open(card, role, prompt)` only ever calls that closure on the fresh path;
+  a resume skips it entirely, because a resumed session already has this).
+  Both now open with `first_prompt(\`public/framework/ai/${card}\`)` — the
+  sub-mastermind convention that a card's id IS its directory under
+  `public/framework/ai/` — before their existing text (the card's transcript
+  for the assistant; the sub-mastermind skill-load line for the manager).
+
+## Left out, on purpose
+
+- **A plain minion** (`role: "minion"` with no `task.dir` and no `page`)
+  carries its own directory in its brief text already — that is the whole
+  point of a brief — so `directory_of()` returns `null` for it and this chain
+  adds nothing. Wiring it in anyway would just repeat what the brief already
+  says.
+- **The mastermind and master-assistant revival spawns** (`Global.js`,
+  `master()` / `mastermind()`) have no directory to name — they are "on duty,
+  answer nothing now" sessions, not bound to any one place — so they are left
+  alone.
+- **The ☰ drawer's page AI** (`ext/drawer/tabs/ai.js`'s `send()`, for a plain
+  page rather than a card) posts to `POST /api/page-ai` — but that route does
+  not exist anywhere in `Servex/` today (checked: no match for `page-ai` under
+  `Servex/`). The client's fetch simply fails and falls back to `ext/Ask`'s
+  dev-server bridge, a separate mechanism entirely (a headless `claude -p`
+  turn run by the dev server, not `Agents.spawn`/`Layers.js`) that this task's
+  fence does not reach. **There is nothing to wire today.** When `/api/page-ai`
+  is built, it should open its fresh agent the same way the card assistant
+  does: `first_prompt(page)` (the page's own URL path — `directory_of()`
+  already maps that shape onto a repo dir) prepended to its first message.
+
+## The proof
+
+A private Servex, a test agent spawned with
+`task: {dir: "public/framework/ux/Dictate"}`: its captured first message opens
+with the root readme, then `public/framework/readme.md`, then
+`public/framework/ux/readme.md`, then `public/framework/ux/Dictate/readme.md`
+— in that order (`public/` itself has no readme, so it is skipped). See
+`public/framework/ai/2026-09-29/readme-chain/proof/`.
+
+## Tests
+
+`node Servex/agents/readme-chain.test.mjs` — plain node, no framework, matching
+`groups.test.mjs`'s house pattern.
